dict(
slot="Diaspora", label="DIASPORA VOICE", cats=[8, 12],
slug="south-africa-231850-foreign-workers-employment-services-bill",
title="South Africa Counts 231,850 Foreign Workers as Tighter Hiring Rules Advance",
excerpt="Labour inspectors recorded 231,850 foreign nationals at inspected workplaces in the first half of 2026/27, as a bill that would let the minister cap foreign hiring by sector moves through Parliament.",
kw="South Africa foreign workers, Employment Services Amendment Bill, foreign employment quotas, African migrants South Africa, labour inspections",
seo_title="South Africa Counts 231,850 Foreign Workers in Inspections",
desc="South African inspectors found 231,850 foreign nationals at inspected workplaces as a bill to cap foreign hiring advances. What it means for African migrants.",
tags=["South Africa","African Diaspora","Migrant Workers","Employment Services Amendment Bill","Labour Migration","Department of Employment and Labour","Immigration Policy","SADC","Jobs","Unemployment","Diaspora Voice","Africa"],
html=f"""
<p><strong>JOHANNESBURG</strong> — South Africa's labour inspectors recorded 231,850 foreign nationals working at the workplaces they visited in the first half of the 2026/27 financial year, figures that have reopened a heated debate over jobs and migration in Africa's most industrialised economy.</p>

<p>The Department of Employment and Labour published the numbers on November 12, <a href="https://africa.businessinsider.com/local/lifestyle/south-africa-discovers-over-200000-foreign-workers-as-ramaphosa-moves-to-tighten/20yq2r8" {A}>Business Insider Africa reported</a>. Inspectors visited 168,766 workplaces nationwide and found that 21,371 of them employed foreign nationals.</p>

<h2>Where foreign workers were found</h2>

<p>Agriculture recorded the largest number, with 46,533 foreign workers across 1,205 employers, followed by wholesale and retail with 41,359 across 6,698 employers, according to the department's Inspector-General of Inspection and Enforcement Services, Aggy Moiloa, <a href="https://www.yfm.co.za/2026/11/13/more-than-231000-foreign-nationals-found-working-in-sa-as-unemployment-remains-high/" {A}>YFM reported</a>. Gauteng had the highest provincial total at 59,624, ahead of North West (58,533) and the Western Cape (34,675).</p>

<p>Moiloa said many of the workers held entry-level positions that could potentially be filled by South Africans. The figures do not say how many of the workers were documented and how many were not.</p>

<h2>A tense jobs backdrop</h2>

<p>The release lands as Statistics South Africa reports unemployment of 33.7% in the third quarter of 2026, with an estimated 8.6 million people out of work, according to YFM. African Transformation Movement leader Vuyo Zungula called for stronger enforcement against employers "who exploit foreign nationals and sideline citizens."</p>

<p>The government says it is recruiting 10,000 additional labour inspectors under a phased programme. President Cyril Ramaphosa has said employers who knowingly hire undocumented workers should face tougher penalties, including possible imprisonment, Business Insider Africa reported.</p>

<h2>What the new bill would change</h2>

<p>The <a href="https://www.labour.gov.za/Media-Desk/Media-Statements/Pages/Putting-South-Africans-first-while-protecting-workers%E2%80%99-rights--Employment-Services-Amendment-Bill-charts-a-balanced-path-fo.aspx" {A}>Employment Services Amendment Bill</a>, presented to Parliament's labour committee, would require employers to verify that a foreign national may work legally, show efforts to recruit qualified South Africans, keep prescribed records and run skills-transfer plans where needed. It would also let the minister, after consulting the Employment Services Board, set maximum quotas for foreign workers in specific sectors, occupations or regions through the Government Gazette.</p>

<p>Proposed fines rise from R100,000 for a first offence to up to R1 million or 10% of a company's gross income for a third. The department says the bill is not aimed at denying migrants their rights: workers employed unlawfully would keep the right to pursue wage and labour claims against employers. "We are trying to strike a balance: to prioritise South Africans, while ensuring that those who are legally in the country and qualify in terms of our laws are also given an opportunity," Employment and Labour Minister Nomakhosazana Meth said.</p>

<h2>What it means for African readers</h2>

<p>South Africa hosts large communities from Zimbabwe, Mozambique, Lesotho, Malawi, Nigeria and beyond. For documented workers, the practical message is to keep visas and permits current and to keep copies of contracts and payslips, because employers will face stricter checks. Businesses that hire across borders should expect more paperwork and audits. The measures are not a blanket ban, and quotas would only take effect if the bill passes and the minister publishes sector limits.</p>

<h2>What to watch</h2>

<ul>
<li>The outcome of public submissions on the bill and its next committee stage.</li>
<li>Any first quota determinations and which sectors they cover.</li>
<li>Responses from SADC governments and migrant-rights groups.</li>
</ul>
"""),

