dict(
slot="Security", label="SECURITY NEWS", cats=[17],
slug="maileva-la-poste-cyberattack-22-million-exposed",
title="La Poste's Maileva Breach Exposed 22 Million People as Paris Cybercrime Probe Opens",
excerpt="A September cyberattack on Maileva, La Poste's business-mail platform, exposed the names and postal addresses of 22 million people. Paris prosecutors confirmed a cybercrime investigation on Sunday.",
kw="Maileva data breach, La Poste cyberattack, supply chain attack, phishing risk, data protection",
seo_title="Maileva Breach Exposes 22 Million in La Poste Cyberattack",
desc="A cyberattack on La Poste subsidiary Maileva exposed names and addresses of 22 million people. How the supplier breach happened and lessons for African firms.",
tags=["Data Breach","Cyberattack","La Poste","Maileva","Docaposte","France","CNIL","Supply Chain Attack","Phishing","Data Protection","Security News","Cybersecurity"],
html=f"""
<p><strong>PARIS</strong> — A French anti-cybercrime unit is investigating a cyberattack on Maileva, a La Poste group subsidiary, that exposed the contact details of 22 million people, the Paris prosecutor's office confirmed on Sunday, November 15.</p>

<p>The attack took place in mid-September but its scale only became public on Saturday, when La Poste confirmed it to Agence France-Presse, <a href="https://www.rfi.fr/en/france/20261116-french-cybercrime-unit-probes-massive-cyberattack-on-la-poste-subsidiary" {A}>RFI reported</a>. The judicial investigation is for an "attack on an automated data processing system."</p>

<h2>What was taken</h2>

<p>Maileva sends business mail for companies, including payslips, registered letters, invoices and notices of general meetings, and handles post for social-housing landlords and hospitals. It is a subsidiary of Docaposte, itself part of La Poste, and claims more than 70,000 users and 180 million documents sent in 2024.</p>

<p>The exposed data covers the first names, surnames and postal addresses of "recipients of mail from Maileva's client companies," La Poste said in a statement to AFP, according to <a href="https://www.letemps.ch/en/cyber/cybersecurity/subsidiary-of-france-s-la-poste-suffered-major-cyberattack-in-september-more-than-20-million-people-affected" {A}>Le Temps</a>. Maileva said bank details were not affected. It has notified France's data protection authority, the CNIL, filed a complaint and informed all affected client companies. La Poste has not described how the attackers got in, and no group has been publicly identified as responsible in the reports reviewed.</p>

<h2>Why a supplier breach matters</h2>

<p>"This is a classic cyberattack scenario, in which hackers attacked a service provider with the aim of recovering databases," Antoine Hautin, secretary general of cybersecurity firm Almond, told Franceinfo, as quoted by RFI. "Maileva is a supplier that presents a systemic risk because it is used by tens of thousands of organisations."</p>

<p>Hautin warned that the main danger now is fraud: messages that look like legitimate letters, which artificial intelligence makes easier to automate at scale. Because the data links individuals to specific senders, such as a hospital or housing provider, criminals can craft convincing lures.</p>

<h2>A run of French incidents</h2>

<p>La Poste was hit by a disruptive attack claimed by pro-Russian hackers in December 2025. Crédit Mutuel Arkéa said this month it had been targeted in an incident involving about €100 million, and scores of French supermarkets were hit in October by attacks that disrupted refrigeration, RFI reported. "We are experiencing a surge in cyberattacks," Public Accounts Minister David Amiel said on Sunday.</p>

<p>The Maileva disclosure follows last week's confirmation by Expedia of a breach through a third-party data platform, another reminder that attackers increasingly target vendors that hold data for many organisations at once.</p>

<h2>What it means for African readers</h2>

<p>African banks, telecoms, insurers and public agencies increasingly outsource payslips, statements and customer notices to bulk-mail, SMS and email platforms. Under laws such as Nigeria's Data Protection Act, Kenya's Data Protection Act and South Africa's POPIA, the organisation that collects the data stays accountable when a supplier is breached. Security teams should map which vendors hold customer data, demand breach-notification clauses and audit rights, and warn customers that genuine institutions will not ask for PINs, passwords or one-time codes by letter, SMS or email.</p>

<h2>What to watch</h2>

<ul>
<li>Any CNIL action or findings on Maileva's security controls.</li>
<li>Claims by a criminal group or sale of the data online.</li>
<li>Phishing campaigns that impersonate Maileva clients.</li>
</ul>
"""),

