dict(
slot="AI", label="ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE", cats=[2, 642],
slug="cassava-720-million-nvidia-ai-plan-africa-huang-exit",
title="Cassava Presses On With $720m Nvidia AI Plan for Africa After Huang's Exit",
excerpt="Strive Masiyiwa's Cassava Technologies, Nvidia's most prominent African partner, is continuing its $720m plan to deploy 12,000 GPUs across five countries after Jensen Huang resigned as Nvidia CEO.",
kw="Cassava Technologies AI, Nvidia Africa, Strive Masiyiwa, AI factory Africa, GPUs in Africa",
seo_title="Cassava Presses On With $720m Nvidia AI Plan for Africa",
desc="Cassava Technologies is pressing on with its $720m plan for 12,000 Nvidia GPUs in Africa after Jensen Huang's exit. What it means for African AI compute.",
tags=["Cassava Technologies","Nvidia","Strive Masiyiwa","Jensen Huang","AI Infrastructure","GPUs","Data Centres","AI Factory","Cape Town","Colette Kress","Artificial Intelligence","Africa Tech"],
html=f"""
<p>Strive Masiyiwa's Cassava Technologies is pressing on with its US$720 million plan to build artificial intelligence data centres across Africa, even after Jensen Huang, the Nvidia co-founder who championed the partnership, resigned as the chipmaker's chief executive on November 12.</p>

<p>Cassava is Nvidia's most prominent partner on the continent and the first Nvidia Cloud Partner headquartered in Africa, <a href="https://www.billionaires.africa/2026/11/14/zimbabwean-billionaire-strive-masiyiwas-720-million-ai-plan-tested-by-jensen-huangs-exit/" {A}>Billionaires.Africa reported</a>. Nvidia is both a supplier and a shareholder, providing the graphics processors for Cassava's "AI factories" and holding an undisclosed stake. Google is also a shareholder.</p>

<h2>What has been built</h2>

<p>Cassava unveiled the plan in March 2025 with a target of putting 12,000 Nvidia GPUs to work in Africa. Masiyiwa said in November 2025 that all 12,000 chips had been bought and flown in. The first AI factory, near Cape Town, runs 3,000 of them. Further sites are planned in Nigeria, Kenya, Egypt and Morocco over the next few years, according to Billionaires.Africa.</p>

<p>Africa still holds less than 0.1% of the world's GPUs, the outlet noted. Masiyiwa has said the continent was once told it had only 80 Nvidia GPUs in total.</p>

<h2>Why Huang's departure matters</h2>

<p>Huang stepped down as president, CEO and board member with immediate effect, ending a 33-year run. Chief Financial Officer Colette Kress is interim chief executive while the board searches for a successor, and Nvidia's board has said it expects operations, customer commitments and its product roadmap to continue without interruption. Kress leads her first earnings call when Nvidia reports third-quarter results on November 17.</p>

<p>Three weeks before he resigned, Huang singled out Egypt and other African markets in an interview with Yahoo Finance. "There's a reason why we're in Africa," he said. "We want to compete everywhere," <a href="https://techcabal.com/2026/11/12/jensen-huang-exit-puts-nvidia-africa-test/" {A}>TechCabal reported</a>.</p>

<p>TechCabal said it is too early to judge the effect on the Cassava deal. The key questions are whether Nvidia's next leadership keeps taking equity stakes in regional operators and gives them timely access to newer chips. If Nvidia becomes more selective, TechCabal said, African operators may have to lean more on outside financing or sign large customers before expanding.</p>

<h2>Others building on Nvidia in Africa</h2>

<p>Cassava is not alone. South Africa's Altron says its Nvidia-powered facility went live with customers including Lelapa AI, which builds speech tools for languages such as isiZulu and Sesotho. In Egypt, Cassava is working with Vodacom and Elsewedy Electric on an AI data centre, and a consortium including Nvidia and South Korea's Naver plans a facility near Casablanca of up to 500 megawatts, according to the two reports.</p>

<h2>What it means for African readers</h2>

<p>Local GPU capacity lets African startups, banks and researchers train and run AI models closer to home, with more control over where data is processed and potentially lower latency and costs. But the supply chain still runs through US hardware makers. For founders, the practical takeaway is to treat local compute as one option among several, compare prices with global clouds, and watch whether Cassava's Lagos, Nairobi, Cairo and Casablanca sites open on schedule.</p>

<h2>What to watch</h2>

<ul>
<li>Nvidia's November 17 results and any comment on international partners.</li>
<li>The choice of a permanent Nvidia CEO.</li>
<li>Launch dates for Cassava's next AI factories outside South Africa.</li>
</ul>
"""),

